order strips and some are internal dividers. Inside the field there are two kinds of thing:\n\n- **Ships.** These are bodies of colour 8 that contain a 9 \"core\". Single cells of other colours (1, 2, 3, 4, 7 …) are attached to the body as markers.\n- **Targets.** These are loose 9 blobs that touch no 8.\n\n**Evidence from the demonstrations.**\n\n- **Pair 1, first ship.** Its core is a single 9. It has a 4-marker on top and two 2-markers on the bottom. In the output it sits on the lone 9 target at (3,18). It has been rotated so that the 4-marker points left, toward the 4 wall at columns 12–13. The 2-markers now point right, toward the 2 wall at columns 24–25.\n- **Pair 1, second ship.** Its core is a horizontal pair of 9s. It moves onto the vertical 9 pair at (5–6, 9). Its 1-markers now face the 1 wall on the left, and its 4-marker faces the 4 wall on the right.\n- **Pair 2.** The same thing happens. The 2-markers face the top 2 wall, the 4-markers face the left 4 wall, and 7 and 3 face their own walls.\n- **What happens to the old cells.** The ships' original cells are erased. Each target 9 is replaced by the ship's own core.\n\n**The rule.**\n\n1. **Find the walls.** A wall is a single-colour component that touches the grid border.\n2. **Find the ships.** Flood from the 8 cells, 4-connected, through every non-background, non-wall cell. That collects the core and the markers. A marker's direction is the step from the body cell next to it out to the marker.\n3. **Find the targets.** These are the 9 cells left over, grouped 8-connected, since a core can be diagonal as in test 1.\n4. **Erase.** Clear every ship and target to background, keeping only the walls.\n5. **Place each ship.**\n   - Try the 4 rotations first, then the 4 reflections.\n   - A transform is allowed only if the transformed core exactly matches the shape of an unused target. The match also fixes the translation.\n   - Score each candidate by casting a ray from every marker in its transformed direction. A marker counts when its ray hits a wall of its own colour.\n   - Keep the best-scoring placement and stamp it.\n\n**The test inputs.** The cores pair up uniquely with targets of matching shape:\n\n- **Test 1:** a 1-cell core goes to the target at (20,20), a diagonal pair to (25,4)/(26,5), and a 2×2 core to (18–19, 7–8).\n- **Test 2:** a 2×2 core goes to (17–18, 4–5), a vertical pair to (24–25, 5), and a single 9 to (6,12).\n\nIn each case the marker rays choose the orientation.\n\n**Teaching stages.** The program emits the erased board first, then one canvas after each ship is stamped.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    bg = background(g)\n    # walls: single-colour components touching the border\n    wall = np.zeros_like(g, dtype=bool)\n    for c in set(np.unique(g)) - {bg}:\n        for comp in components(g == c):\n            if any(y in (0, H-1) or x in (0, W-1) for y, x in comp):\n                for y, x in comp:\n                    wall[y, x] = True\n    free = (g != bg) & ~wall\n    # ships: flood from 8 cells through free cells (4-conn)\n    seen = np.zeros_like(free)\n    ships = []\n    for y in range(H):\n        for x in range(W):\n            if g[y, x] == 8 and not seen[y, x]:\n                stack = [(y, x)]; seen[y, x] = True; cells = []\n                while stack:\n                    cy, cx = stack.pop(); cells.append((cy, cx))\n                    for dy, dx in ((1,0),(-1,0),(0,1),(0,-1)):\n                        ny, nx = cy+dy, cx+dx\n                        if 0 <= ny < H and 0 <= nx < W and free[ny, nx] and not seen[ny, nx]:\n                            seen[ny, nx] = True; stack.append((ny, nx))\n                ships.append(cells)\n    tmask = (g == 9) & ~seen\n    targets = [set(c) for c in components(tmask, diagonal=True)]\n    canvas = g.copy()\n    canvas[free] = bg\n    emit(canvas, \"Erase all ships and loose 9 targets, keeping only the walls.\")\n\n    def norm(cells):\n        my = min(y for y, x in cells); mx = min(x for y, x in cells)\n        return frozenset((y-my, x-mx) for y, x in cells), (my, mx)\n\n    transforms = [lambda y, x: (y, x), lambda y, x: (x, -y), lambda y, x: (-y, -x), lambda y, x: (-x, y),\n                  lambda y, x: (y, -x), lambda y, x: (-x, -y), lambda y, x: (-y, x), lambda y, x: (x, y)]\n    used = set()\n    for cells in ships:\n        col = {c: g[c] for c in cells}\n        best = None\n        for ti, T in enumerate(transforms):\n            tc = {T(y, x): col[(y, x)] for y, x in cells}\n            core = [p for p, v in tc.items() if v == 9]\n            if not core:\n                continue\n            cn, (cy0, cx0) = norm(core)\n            for k, t in enumerate(targets):\n                if k in used:\n                    continue\n                tn, (ty0, tx0) = norm(t)\n                if tn != cn:\n                    continue\n                dy, dx = ty0 - cy0, tx0 - cx0\n                placed = {(y+dy, x+dx): v for (y, x), v in tc.items()}\n                if any(not (0 <= y < H and 0 <= x < W) for y, x in placed):\n                    continue\n                score = 0\n                for (y, x), v in placed.items():\n                    if v in (8, 9):\n                        continue\n                    for sy, sx in ((1,0),(-1,0),(0,1),(0,-1)):\n                        if placed.get((y-sy, x-sx)) in (8, 9):\n                            ry, rx = y+sy, x+sx\n                            while 0 <= ry < H and 0 <= rx < W and not wall[ry, rx]:\n                                ry += sy; rx += sx\n                            if 0 <= ry < H and 0 <= rx < W and g[ry, rx] == v:\n                                score += 1\n                            break\n                key = (score, -ti)\n                if best is None or key > best[0]:\n                    best = (key, k, placed)\n        if best is None:\n            continue\n        _, k, placed = best\n        used.add(k)\n        for (y, x), v in placed.items():\n            canvas[y, x] = v\n        emit(canvas, \"Rotate the ship so each marker faces its same-coloured wall and stamp it onto the matching 9 target.\")\n    emit(canvas, \"All ships docked on their targets.\")\n    return canvas\n","candidate_tactics":{"objects":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Find the ships.** Flood from the 8 cells, 4-connected, through every non-background, non-wall cell."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Find the targets.** These are the 9 cells left over, grouped 8-connected, since a core can be diagonal as in test 1."}],"panels":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Some walls are border strips and some are internal dividers."}],"legend":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"It has been rotated so that the 4-marker points left, toward the 4 wall at columns 12–13."}],"rigid":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"It has been rotated so that the 4-marker points left, toward the 4 wall at columns 12–13."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Try the 4 rotations first, then the 4 reflections."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Rotate the ship so each marker faces its same-coloured wall and stamp it onto the matching 9 target."}],"movement":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The match also fixes the translation."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"All ships docked on their targets."}],"template":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Keep the best-scoring placement and stamp it."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"**The test inputs.** The cores pair up uniquely with targets of matching shape:"},{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Teaching stages.** The program emits the erased board first, then one canvas after each ship is stamped."}],"topology":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Find the ships.** Flood from the 8 cells, 4-connected, through every non-background, non-wall cell."}],"rays":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Score each candidate by casting a ray from every marker in its transformed direction."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"A marker counts when its ray hits a wall of its own colour."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"In each case the marker rays choose the orientation."}],"count":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"A marker counts when its ray hits a wall of its own colour."}],"layers":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"It has a 4-marker on top and two 2-markers on the bottom."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"250f90ec6b1fb08cfc638d55575e72a1310df10f75d740d1fabb4619bef1ecf4","task_sha256":"231b463d120f2a71b6c42d2d8ddb5612025df85b28365d7195005fd951eb39d6","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
